<h2 id="bg-ill-01">BG-ILL-01: Use an illustration only when it explains something</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to new explanatory illustrations and dashboard imagery.

Decide the message and the task first. An illustration can explain where to put a sensor, how a phone relates to a hub, or a setup step that words alone make hard. Put the full instruction beside it.

Do not fill an empty dashboard card with decoration. Do not use a cheerful picture to suggest a device is online, or a comforting scene to replace a camera view. Choose an operational image, a functional icon or a chart when the task needs one. [Icons and images](images.html) owns those.


<h2 id="bg-ill-02">BG-ILL-02: Draw original, simple civica artwork</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to new civica explanatory artwork.

Use flat geometric shapes, little detail and one clear focal point. Keep each illustration recognisable at the size it is shown. Draw from real objects where accuracy matters and remove details that do not explain the action.

Do not copy GOV.UK characters, its crown, its dot-derived graphic device or its identity compositions. Circles and rectangles are drawing tools, not permission to reproduce another identity. Use supplied identity artwork unchanged and only in its proper context. civica identity belongs to project documentation and promotion. A household interface does not need it. The examples on this page are original schematic SVGs.


<h2 id="bg-ill-03">BG-ILL-03: Use a restrained palette without borrowing status</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to explanatory illustration family, excluding functional icons and data marks.

Start with the selected civica primary, a neutral canvas and a few supporting shades. Four to six colours is an upper starting point for a complex scene, not a target. A simple diagram may need fewer. Check neighbouring shapes and essential edges against the real background.

Do not use a warning or success colour to decorate a scene that shows no such state. Avoid gradients, textures, outlines and 3D effects in this illustration family. Functional icon strokes and chart hatching stay, because their own specifications require them. Do not remove accessibility cues from controls or data in the name of restraint.


<h2 id="bg-ill-04">BG-ILL-04: Compose around the action</h2>

**Local recommendation: SHOULD.** Applies to composition and sequencing of explanatory illustrations.

Use whitespace, scale and a little overlap to make the subject clear. A frame or background shape may set the scene, but it must not hide a hand, a device label or the action. Keep the illustration and its text together on narrow screens.

A sequence of panels can explain several steps. Give each panel a readable order and matching text. Do not add movement just to attract attention. If a process needs animation, provide a static version, a playback control where needed, and respect reduced motion.


<h2 id="bg-ill-05">BG-ILL-05: Represent people and gestures carefully</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to illustrated people, gestures and avatars.

If people help explain the task, draw believable proportions and show a range of age, skin tone, body type, clothing and disability. Do not assume who owns the home or who does a chore. Do not use an avatar’s look to suggest identity, gender or capability.

A hand that points to a control must point to the right one. Sign language, safety instructions and accessibility gestures need review by someone who knows the subject. A generic geometric hand cannot stand in for them. civica does not specify a character system.


<h2 id="bg-ill-06">BG-ILL-06: Give the image the right alternative</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to informative, redundant and decorative image alternatives.

For an informative image, describe what the surrounding text does not already say. A complex diagram also needs a nearby explanation or a structured alternative. Keep labels and instructions as HTML where you can. [Images](../content/formatting-content/images.html) owns the wording.

If an image only repeats the text beside it, give it an empty alternative, or hide a redundant inline SVG from assistive technology. Do not leave out the `alt` attribute on an `<img>` because the image is decorative. Give an informative inline SVG an accessible name, and stop its decorative shapes becoming extra stops for a screen reader.


<h2 id="bg-ill-07">BG-ILL-07: Keep icons functional and recognisable</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to functional entity/domain and navigation icons.

Use Home Assistant’s own entity and domain icons, with their state variants, for repeated household controls. Pair each icon with a name and a state. Give an icon-only action an accessible name and a large enough target. Keep manufacturer artwork when it identifies the device, without implying the manufacturer owns civica.

Do not turn native icons into faceless illustrations or replace a meaningful outline icon with a solid blob. The action-link arrow and native 24px symbols are functional vectors. Their strokes, alignment and focus follow their component specification, not this illustration family.


<h2 id="bg-ill-08">BG-ILL-08: Preserve photographs, video and map evidence</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to photos, media, cameras, map tiles and illustrative stand-ins.

Room photos, device images, media artwork, camera feeds and map tiles are operational content with their own purpose and rights. Do not flatten, recolour or crop out identifying detail to make them look like an illustration. Give a camera image its location, whether it is available and when it last updated. A still frame does not prove a live stream.

Show unavailable media as unavailable and keep the native player or camera controls. Put text and controls over an image on a tested solid surface where possible. An illustration may explain how a camera works if you label it as a diagram. It must not stand in for the camera’s real state.


<h2 id="bg-ill-09">BG-ILL-09: Limit personal information in shared imagery</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to public examples and personal household imagery.

Use invented household details in public examples. Before you publish a screenshot, remove access tokens, exact home coordinates, faces, number plates and any names the task does not need. Get permission for identifiable people and for photos someone else supplied. Record who holds the rights. A photo being public is not permission.

Presence avatars and camera previews are personal content. Keep their access controls and Home Assistant’s privacy choices. Do not add faces, guess identities or show a precise location to make a dashboard feel more personal.


<h2 id="bg-ill-10">BG-ILL-10: Deliver and review the complete asset</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to new asset delivery, rights metadata and rendered review.

Keep the editable source, author, licence, purpose, alternative-text decision and intended background with each new asset (see `docs/data/illustration-assets.json`). Deliver a web format: SVG for simple vectors, a suitable raster format for photographs. Do not make people view an EPS or AI production file. Optimise the file without losing meaningful detail.

Check the rendered image at small and large sizes, in light and dark contexts, with images switched off and, where useful, with a colour-vision simulation. A successful SVG parse is not a usability or accessibility pass. Record remaining limitations and test evidence in the checklist.


## Review and evidence

The rule inventory and checklist are `docs/data/brand-guidance-inventory.json` and `docs/data/brand-guidance-checklist.json`. Reviewing a source is not a pass for an interface. Before you close a checklist item, record the page or component, theme and mode, test result and any remaining issue. Recheck after a palette, component or renderer change.
